      { type: 'intro', text: 'FVGs are not all interchangeable, but calling one valid needs a written definition. A three-candle imbalance can be tagged by size, displacement, timeframe or location; none of those labels alone demonstrates institutional significance or guarantees a reliable reaction.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Criterion 1 — The Displacement Requirement' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A displacement filter can be defined using measurable candle-size or overlap rules. Set thresholds before reviewing the sample and use the same values across all eligible examples; visual impressions of aggressive movement can otherwise become hindsight selection.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A visual review can help identify candidate formations, but words such as genuine or noise need an observable definition. Predefine candle-size, overlap or relative-volatility thresholds, apply them to every candidate and test whether the filter improves results; appearance alone does not verify participants or predictive value.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Criterion 2 — Higher Timeframe Alignment' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some strategies use a higher-timeframe filter when evaluating a candidate FVG. A bullish 5-minute FVG during bearish 4-hour structure is a conflicting-context example, not automatically invalid or low probability. Test the filter against the same entry rules without it.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Before testing an FVG rule, document any higher-timeframe references or direction filters you intend to use. Alignment alone is not sufficient to establish a high-probability entry; compare results with a simple baseline and include non-aligned setups.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Criterion 3 — The First FVG in the Sequence' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some ICT variants prioritize the first FVG identified after displacement. The candle sequence does not prove that it carries greater institutional significance or that later FVGs have lower probability. Define the selection rule before testing and compare first, later and unfiltered candidates.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Criterion 4 — No Prior Violations' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some FVG models change a zone’s label after price crosses a chosen boundary. Specify whether a wick or close counts, which boundary matters and whether a later revisit is required. A fully crossed FVG is not automatically unusable in every model, nor does the label guarantee that it will act with opposite polarity.' },
      { type: 'list', items: [
        'Check: has price entered the zone under your selected mitigation rule? Record the amount filled, but do not assume a 50% threshold reduces its value unless that rule is part of the model being tested.',
        'Check: has price crossed the boundary that your strategy defines as full mitigation? Apply the chosen rule consistently and measure outcomes rather than applying a universal ban.',
        'Check: does your defined IFVG rule classify this zone after the required violation and confirmation? A polarity change remains a hypothesis to test, not an automatic consequence of crossing the gap.',
      ]},
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Criterion 5 — Session Timing' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some traders compare FVG outcomes by session and market activity. An Asian-session or holiday-period gap is not automatically less valid, and the chart does not verify institutional participation. Define the session and compare the same FVG rules across periods, including costs and no-trade cases.' },
      { type: 'highlight', text: 'A possible FVG checklist can include: (1) a defined size/displacement rule, (2) a prewritten context filter if used, (3) whether the model prioritizes the first FVG, (4) the selected mitigation rule and (5) the recorded session. Treat each condition as model-specific and test whether it adds value rather than making all five universal validity requirements.' },
      {"type":"heading","text":"Worked hypothetical example: screen an FVG with fixed criteria"},
      {"type":"paragraph","text":"A trader sets a minimum FVG width of 0.25 ATR and requires the middle candle's true range to exceed a predefined threshold. A three-candle formation qualifies only if the first and third candle extremes create the required interval and a nearby close meets the confirmation rule. A narrower gap is skipped under the written rule, even if it later produces a visually attractive bounce."},
      {"type":"heading","text":"Failure case and what to test"},
      {"type":"paragraph","text":"Changing minimum width, displacement, timeframe or context can produce very different samples. A selected gap may fail to react or never be revisited. Compare the filter to unfiltered FVGs and avoid describing a gap as valid solely because its eventual path looks successful."},
      {"type":"heading","text":"Test checklist"},
      {"type":"list","items":["Set minimum width and displacement rules before sampling.","Define which context filters are mandatory and how they are measured.","Log every qualifying gap, not only those later touched.","Report rejected entries, failed reactions and results after costs."]}
