    content: [
      { type: 'intro', text: 'Displacement is a term ICT traders use for a relatively strong, directional move. It can be operationalized with rules such as candle-body size, overlap or a close beyond a defined swing, but it does not by itself identify institutional participants.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'What Is a Displacement Move?' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Displacement is an ICT term for a relatively strong directional price move. A trader can operationalize it with candle-body size, overlap, range expansion or a close beyond a selected swing; these are alternative measurable criteria, not a universal definition. Large candles may form for many reasons, and a move does not by itself identify who traded or why price moved.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Possible measurable displacement features include body-to-range ratio, overlap with adjacent candles, range relative to recent bars, or a close beyond a preselected swing. A 60–80% body ratio is one candidate threshold to test, not a universal requirement. Choose the threshold and combination of features before reviewing results.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Displacement as an Observable Price Pattern' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Candle overlap alone cannot distinguish retail activity from institutional execution. Large directional candles can have multiple causes, so define measurable criteria and avoid using the word institutional as a conclusion drawn from candle shape.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some ICT variants require a defined displacement condition before they accept an FVG or Order Block as a setup filter. Other definitions classify a three-candle FVG from candle geometry alone. Neither candle shape nor the absence of a selected displacement threshold reveals whether retail or institutional participants traded there; state the pattern definition separately from any additional filter.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Identifying Displacement — Checklist' },
      { type: 'list', items: [
        'If body-to-range ratio is part of your rule, what threshold did you predefine? Compare alternatives rather than treating 60% as universal.',
        'If candle overlap is part of your rule, measure it consistently; overlap alone does not establish balanced participation or identify who traded.',
        'Does the move cover significantly more price distance per candle than the surrounding price action? Compare the displacement candles to the 10 candles before them.',
        'Does the sequence meet the separately defined FVG rule? A gap between selected candle extremes is observable; its presence does not prove a cause or guarantee a return.',
        'If an Order Block is used as a separate filter, define its candidate candle and boundaries in advance rather than inferring that every displacement begins from one.',
        'Does the displacement begin from a recognizable ICT level — an OB, FVG, swing extreme, or PD Array?',
      ]},
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Displacement and the FVG — The Inseparable Relationship' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Some models require a displacement rule alongside an FVG definition; others identify the FVG from the relationship between selected candle extremes. Define whether displacement is required before testing. A three-candle gap is a chart pattern under that convention, not evidence of institutional activity or a guarantee of predictive value.' },
      { type: 'highlight', text: 'If your model combines an FVG with a displacement filter, write both definitions before collecting examples. Compare the combined rule with the FVG rule alone on the same sample, including missed entries, stop-outs, costs and cases where price never returns. Do not assume the filter improves expectancy until results support that conclusion.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Worked hypothetical example' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A trader defines a candidate displacement as a candle body larger than a specified multiple of the recent median body, followed by a close beyond a confirmed swing. When a large candle occurs inside the prior range and fails the swing-break condition, it is not counted as displacement under this rule.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Failure case and what to test' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Terms such as strong, clean or impulsive are subjective unless the sample, candle size and structure conditions are explicit. Volatility spikes and news can produce large candles that do not create a durable directional move.' },
      { type: 'heading', text: 'Test checklist' },
      { type: 'list', items: [
        'Choose candle-size and swing-break criteria before testing.',
        'Include large candles that fail the remaining conditions.',
        'Compare results with a simpler volatility or momentum threshold.',
      ]},

    ],
